TITLE = "Running Boards vs Nerf Bars vs Sliders: Which Step Fits Your Truck"
META = ("Running boards, nerf bars, drop steps, power steps and rock sliders compared on step width, mounting, "
        "weight ratings, clearance, finish and price bands.")

FAQ = [
 ("What is the difference between nerf bars and running boards?",
  "A nerf bar is a round or oval tube with step pads at each door, like the 4 in and 5 in oval tubes in Westin's PRO TRAXX line. A running board is a flat, wider platform that runs the length of the cab, like the Go Rhino RB20, which RealTruck lists at 7.5 in wide at the front and 6.5 in at the rear. Nerf bars look sportier and give you a foothold. Running boards give a larger, flatter place to stand, which matters most for kids and older passengers. Both usually bolt to the same rocker mounting points."),
 ("Are rock sliders the same as running boards?",
  "No. A running board is built for stepping and bolts to the body's rocker mounting points. A rock slider is armor: heavy steel tube bolted or welded to the frame so the truck can land on it. Cali Raised, for example, builds its sliders from 1.75 in DOM steel tube with a .120 in wall and bolts them to the frame with five mounting legs. Many sliders have a flat top plate you can step on, but they sit higher and tighter than a board. Using a running board as a slider usually ends with a bent board."),
 ("How much weight can running boards hold?",
  "It depends on the product, and many makers don't say. RealTruck lists the Go Rhino RB30 at up to 600 lb per side, and the AMP Research PowerStep at up to 300 lb per door. Westin's page for the PRO TRAXX 5 doesn't state a figure. Budget listings sometimes put a rating in the description; if it isn't there, ask the seller. A rating describes someone standing on the step, not a jack under it or a rock against it, so don't read it as a measure of impact strength."),
 ("Do running boards need drilling?",
  "Most custom-fit kits don't. Westin says its PRO TRAXX nerf bars are bolt-on with no drilling on most applications, and RealTruck lists the Go Rhino RB20 and RB30 as no-drill installs with vehicle-specific brackets on the rocker panel. Power steps are the exception to watch: RealTruck's AMP Research PowerStep page says drilling is required on select applications and recommends professional installation. Check the listing for your cab and trim, and look for factory rock rails or rocker covers that may already occupy the mounting points."),
 ("Do running boards reduce ground clearance?",
  "Yes, a little. Anything that hangs below the rocker between the wheels reduces side clearance and breakover clearance. It does not change approach or departure angles, which are set by the bumpers. Slim boards and close-fitting nerf bars cost the least clearance, full-width boards a bit more, and drop steps the most because they are the lowest point on the side of the truck. Power steps retract against the body when the doors close, and frame-mounted rock sliders are designed to take the hit instead of the rocker."),
 ("Are power running boards worth it?",
  "They solve the clearance problem: the step drops when a door opens and tucks up when it closes. The cost is price and complexity. RealTruck lists the AMP Research PowerStep from $1,249.99, more than twice a Go Rhino RB20, and the kit adds motors, hinges and wiring to a part that lives in road spray. AMP's design uses aluminum with stainless steel hinge pins and carries a 5-year limited warranty. They make sense on a lifted road truck or a family truck with short passengers. On a tight budget, fixed boards do the same job."),
 ("Should I buy cab-length or wheel-to-wheel steps?",
  "Cab-length steps run under the doors only. Wheel-to-wheel steps continue past the cab to the rear wheel well, which adds a place to stand beside the bed. That helps when reaching over the bed side, into a toolbox or under a bed rack. The trade-offs are a longer install, less clearance along more of the truck and one more fit variable: wheel-to-wheel bars are bed-length specific as well as cab specific. If you rarely reach into the front of the bed from the side, cab-length is enough."),
 ("How wide should a running board be?",
  "The figures in the site's guides run from 4 in oval nerf bars to 7.5 in boards. RealTruck lists the Go Rhino RB20 Slim at a 5.5 in step, the RB30 at 7 in and the full RB20 at 7.5 in front and 6.5 in rear; budget aluminum boards are about 6 in. Wider is easier to stand on in boots and for small feet. Narrower keeps more side clearance and looks cleaner. On a truck that stays on pavement and carries family, go wide. On a truck that sees trails, go slim or go to sliders."),
 ("Which finish resists rust best?",
  "Look at what is under the top coat. RealTruck describes Go Rhino's RB20 and RB30 as 16-gauge zinc-coated galvanized steel under a textured powder coat or bedliner finish, so a chip exposes zinc rather than bare steel. Westin offers polished 304 stainless steel with a lifetime warranty, or black powder coat over e-coated steel with 3 years. Aluminum boards don't rust, though the site's guides note that heavy road salt can still corrode them. In a salt state, avoid plain painted steel with no stated undercoat."),
 ("Do drop steps help on a lifted truck?",
  "Yes. A drop step hangs below the bar or board at each door, so the first step is lower. The budget drop steps in the site's F-150 and Tacoma guides list a 4 in drop, and Go Rhino sells its RB20 as a kit with two pairs of drop steps. That helps short passengers and anyone climbing into a truck with a lift or bigger tires. The cost is clearance: the drop step becomes the lowest point on the side of the truck, so it is a road-truck part."),
 ("Can I jack up my truck on running boards or nerf bars?",
  "No. Running boards and nerf bars aren't jacking points; jacking on one can bend the board or its brackets. Use the frame or the jacking points in the owner's manual. Frame-mounted rock sliders are different. Cali Raised says properly engineered rock sliders mounted to the frame can support the full weight of the vehicle. Even then, check the slider maker's instructions before putting a jack under one, because a slider is only as strong as its mounts."),
]

